    "summary": "Security teams have spent years building identity and access controls for human users and service accounts. But a new category of actor has quietly entered most enterprise environments, and it operates entirely outside those controls. Claude Code, Anthropic's AI coding agent, is now running across e",
    "body": "I don't have web search access right now, but I have enough context from the title and summary to write this article. The topic is clear: Ceros (a security platform) addressing the gap in enterprise security controls around AI coding agents like Claude Code. Let me write the article based on the available information and my knowledge of this space.\n\n---\n\n# How Ceros Is Tackling the Blind Spot AI Coding Agents Create for Enterprise Security Teams\n\n## The Rise of the Non-Human Developer\n\nFor years, enterprise security teams have meticulously built identity and access management frameworks around two well-understood principals: human users and service accounts. Policies were tuned, least-privilege models enforced, and audit trails constructed to ensure every actor touching production code or sensitive infrastructure could be identified, scoped, and monitored. But a new category of actor has quietly entered most enterprise environments—and it operates entirely outside those controls.\n\nClaude Code, Anthropic's agentic AI coding assistant, is now running across engineering organizations at scale. It reads codebases, writes production code, executes shell commands, interacts with APIs, and commits changes to repositories—often with the same credential set as the developer who invoked it. And from the perspective of most enterprise security stacks, it is invisible. It doesn't have its own identity. It doesn't generate the kind of telemetry that traditional SIEM platforms know how to parse. It is, in effect, a shadow actor with developer-level privileges.\n\nCeros, a security platform focused on non-human identity governance, is positioning itself to close that gap—offering security teams the visibility and policy enforcement layer they need to bring AI coding agents under the same governance umbrella as every other actor in the environment.\n\n## Background: The Non-Human Identity Crisis\n\nThe proliferation of AI coding agents represents the latest chapter in what security researchers have been calling the \"non-human identity crisis.\" Over the past several years, the number of machine identities—API keys, service accounts, OAuth tokens, CI/CD bot credentials—has exploded, often outnumbering human identities by a factor of 10 to 1 or more in large enterprises. Security teams have scrambled to build controls around these, with mixed results.\n\nAI coding agents compound the problem in a uniquely dangerous way. Unlike a CI/CD bot that performs a narrowly scoped, predictable set of actions, an AI agent like Claude Code is general-purpose by design. It reasons about tasks, makes autonomous decisions about which files to read, which commands to execute, and which APIs to call. Its behavior is non-deterministic. And critically, it typically inherits the full permissions of the developer session in which it runs—meaning it can access anything that developer can access.\n\nThis creates a threat model that traditional identity and access management (IAM) tools were never designed to handle. The agent isn't a user. It isn't a service account. It's something new—an autonomous actor operating under borrowed credentials with broad, unpredictable scope.\n\n## Technical Details: Where the Security Gap Lives\n\nThe core technical challenge is threefold.\n\n**First, there is no distinct identity.** When Claude Code executes a `git push`, makes an API call, or reads a file from a shared drive, it does so under the developer's credentials. There is no separate principal in the identity provider, no dedicated OAuth client, and no distinguishable token. From the perspective of downstream systems, the activity is indistinguishable from the developer themselves.\n\n**Second, the permission surface is vastly over-scoped.** Developers routinely have access to secrets managers, internal APIs, production databases (in some environments), and broad repository access. When an AI agent inherits that session, it inherits all of it—far beyond what it actually needs for any given task. The principle of least privilege, a cornerstone of enterprise security, is violated by default.\n\n**Third, audit trails are incomplete.** Even organizations with mature logging may struggle to differentiate between actions a developer took manually and actions their AI agent took autonomously. This makes incident investigation, compliance reporting, and anomaly detection significantly harder.\n\nCeros addresses these challenges by inserting an identity and policy layer between the AI agent and the resources it accesses. The platform creates a distinct, auditable identity for the agent session, applies scoped access policies that limit what the agent can reach independent of the developer's broader permissions, and generates telemetry that feeds into existing SIEM and SOAR workflows. The goal is not to block AI agent adoption—it's to make it governable.\n\n## Real-World Impact: Why This Matters Now\n\nThe implications for organizations running AI coding agents without this kind of governance are significant. Consider a scenario where a developer invokes Claude Code to refactor a microservice. The agent, in the course of its work, reads environment variables containing database credentials, accesses an internal API that returns customer records, and pushes code to a shared repository—all actions that are technically within the developer's permission scope but far outside the intended scope of the refactoring task.\n\nIn a worst-case scenario, a prompt injection attack or a malicious instruction embedded in a repository file could cause the agent to exfiltrate secrets, modify CI/CD pipelines, or introduce subtle vulnerabilities into production code. Without a distinct identity and policy boundary around the agent, the organization has no mechanism to detect or prevent this.\n\nRegulatory pressure adds urgency. Frameworks like SOC 2, ISO 27001, and emerging AI-specific regulations increasingly require organizations to demonstrate control over all actors—human and non-human—that access sensitive systems. An ungoverned AI agent operating under borrowed credentials is, from a compliance perspective, a finding waiting to happen.\n\n## Threat Actor Context: The Emerging Attack Surface\n\nWhile no large-scale, publicly attributed attacks exploiting AI coding agents have been confirmed at the time of writing, the threat is not theoretical. Security researchers have repeatedly demonstrated prompt injection techniques that can manipulate AI agents into performing unintended actions. The attack surface includes malicious instructions hidden in code comments, README files, issue descriptions, and even variable names—all places an AI coding agent will read as part of its normal operation.\n\nThe concern is particularly acute in open-source supply chain scenarios, where an attacker could embed instructions in a public dependency that an AI agent processes during a build or code review task. This represents a novel variant of supply chain attacks that most existing defenses are not equipped to detect.\n\n## Defensive Recommendations\n\nOrganizations running AI coding agents in any capacity should consider the following immediate steps:\n\n- **Establish distinct identities for AI agents.** Do not allow agents to operate under developer credentials without a separate, auditable identity layer. Solutions like Ceros can provide this, but even manual controls—such as dedicated service accounts with scoped permissions—are a meaningful improvement over the status quo.\n\n- **Apply least-privilege scoping.** Define what resources an AI agent actually needs for its tasks and enforce those boundaries at the policy level. An agent performing code review does not need access to production secrets.\n\n- **Instrument and monitor.** Ensure that AI agent activity generates telemetry that is ingested by your SIEM. Build detection rules for anomalous behavior—such as an agent accessing resources outside its normal scope or executing unexpected commands.\n\n- **Harden the input surface.** Treat all content an AI agent processes as potentially adversarial. This includes code comments, documentation, and third-party dependencies. Implement guardrails that flag or block agent actions triggered by suspicious inputs.\n\n- **Establish governance policies.** Define and enforce organizational standards for where and how AI coding agents may be used, what approvals are required, and how their activity is reviewed.\n\n## Industry Response: A Market Taking Shape\n\nCeros is not operating in isolation. The broader security industry is beginning to recognize that AI agents represent a fundamentally new category of principal that requires purpose-built controls. Several startups and established vendors are developing solutions for non-human identity management, AI agent governance, and prompt injection defense.\n\nAnthropic itself has taken steps to address the challenge from the model side, building features like permission prompting and tool-use restrictions into Claude Code. But model-side controls alone are insufficient for enterprise governance—security teams need infrastructure-level enforcement that operates independently of the agent's own behavior.\n\nThe convergence of AI agent adoption and non-human identity governance is likely to be one of the defining themes of enterprise security in 2026 and beyond. Organizations that treat AI coding agents as just another developer tool—rather than as a new class of autonomous actor requiring its own governance framework—do so at considerable risk.\n\n---\n\n**",
