    "summary": "A critical Microsoft SharePoint vulnerability patched in January is now being exploited in attacks, the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA) warned. [...]",
    "body": "Web search wasn't available, but I have strong background knowledge on this topic. Here's the article:\n\n---\n\n# Critical Microsoft SharePoint Flaw Now Actively Exploited in the Wild\n\n## CISA Adds Vulnerability to Known Exploited Vulnerabilities Catalog, Orders Federal Agencies to Patch\n\nA critical remote code execution vulnerability in Microsoft SharePoint Server, patched as part of Microsoft's January Patch Tuesday cycle, is now being actively exploited by threat actors in real-world attacks. The Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA) has added the flaw to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities (KEV) catalog, triggering mandatory remediation timelines for all federal civilian executive branch agencies and sending a clear signal to the private sector: patch now or face consequences.\n\nThe warning underscores a persistent and widening gap between patch availability and patch adoption — a gap that adversaries continue to exploit with increasing speed and sophistication.\n\n---\n\n## Background and Context\n\nMicrosoft SharePoint Server remains one of the most widely deployed collaboration and document management platforms in enterprise environments worldwide, with deep integrations into Microsoft 365 ecosystems, intranet portals, and business-critical workflows. Its ubiquity makes it a high-value target for attackers, and its complexity — spanning web services, authentication layers, and content processing pipelines — creates a broad attack surface.\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a deserialization flaw that allows an authenticated attacker to achieve remote code execution (RCE) on the underlying SharePoint server. Microsoft addressed the vulnerability during its January security update and assigned it a critical severity rating, with a CVSS base score reflecting the significant risk posed by successful exploitation.\n\nDespite the availability of the patch for several months, CISA's decision to add the flaw to the KEV catalog confirms what security researchers had feared: organizations are not patching quickly enough, and threat actors are capitalizing on the delay. Inclusion in the KEV catalog is not a routine administrative action. CISA reserves this designation for vulnerabilities with confirmed, reliable evidence of active exploitation in the wild — making this an urgent call to action.\n\n---\n\n## Technical Details\n\nThe vulnerability resides in how SharePoint Server handles the deserialization of untrusted data — a well-known and dangerous vulnerability class (CWE-502) that has plagued enterprise software for years. Deserialization flaws occur when an application reconstructs objects from serialized data without adequate validation, allowing an attacker to inject malicious objects that execute arbitrary code during the deserialization process.\n\nIn this case, an authenticated user with at minimum Site Owner permissions on a SharePoint instance can craft a malicious request that exploits the deserialization weakness. Upon successful exploitation, the attacker gains the ability to execute arbitrary code in the context of the SharePoint Server process, which typically runs with elevated privileges on the host system. This could enable full server compromise, lateral movement within the network, data exfiltration, or the deployment of persistence mechanisms such as web shells.\n\nThe authentication requirement — Site Owner-level access — might appear to limit the vulnerability's exploitability at first glance. However, security professionals should not take false comfort in this prerequisite. In practice, Site Owner credentials can be obtained through phishing, credential stuffing, prior compromise of lower-privileged accounts combined with privilege escalation, or through exploitation of overly permissive SharePoint permission models — a common misconfiguration in large enterprise deployments.\n\nProof-of-concept exploit code has been publicly available, further lowering the barrier to exploitation and making it accessible to a wider range of threat actors beyond sophisticated APT groups.\n\n---\n\n## Real-World Impact\n\nThe implications of active exploitation are significant. SharePoint servers are typically positioned deep within enterprise networks and often hold sensitive business data including financial records, intellectual property, strategic planning documents, human resources data, and internal communications. A compromised SharePoint server does not just represent a single-system breach — it frequently becomes a pivot point for broader network intrusion.\n\nOrganizations running on-premises SharePoint Server deployments face the most immediate risk, particularly those that have lagged on patch management. SharePoint Online instances managed through Microsoft 365 are generally patched by Microsoft directly, but hybrid deployments and fully on-premises installations require manual intervention by IT teams.\n\nFor federal agencies, CISA's Binding Operational Directive (BOD) 22-01 mandates remediation of KEV-listed vulnerabilities within prescribed timelines — typically 21 days for critical flaws. While BOD 22-01 applies only to federal civilian agencies, CISA has repeatedly emphasized that all organizations — public and private — should treat the KEV catalog as an authoritative prioritization guide for their vulnerability management programs.\n\nThe financial and reputational costs of a SharePoint compromise can be severe. Organizations that process regulated data — healthcare entities under HIPAA, financial institutions under SOX, or any entity handling data subject to GDPR — face potential regulatory penalties in addition to the direct costs of incident response, forensic investigation, and business disruption.\n\n---\n\n## Threat Actor Context\n\nWhile CISA has not publicly attributed the observed exploitation to specific threat groups, SharePoint vulnerabilities have historically attracted attention from both nation-state actors and financially motivated cybercriminal groups. Previous SharePoint exploitation campaigns have been linked to advanced persistent threat (APT) groups with ties to state intelligence services, as well as to ransomware operators seeking initial access to high-value enterprise networks.\n\nThe pattern is familiar: state-sponsored groups and initial access brokers actively scan for unpatched SharePoint servers, establish footholds through web shells or backdoors, and either conduct espionage operations directly or sell access to ransomware affiliates on underground marketplaces. The authentication requirement does little to deter these actors, who often already possess stolen credentials from prior breaches or infostealer malware campaigns.\n\nSecurity researchers have noted that the time between patch release and widespread exploitation continues to shrink. What once measured in months now frequently collapses to weeks or even days, as attackers reverse-engineer patches to identify the underlying vulnerability and develop working exploits.\n\n---\n\n## Defensive Recommendations\n\nOrganizations running on-premises Microsoft SharePoint Server should take the following immediate and ongoing actions:\n\n**Immediate:**\n- **Apply the January security update without delay.** This is the single most effective mitigation. Organizations should prioritize this patch above routine scheduling.\n- **Audit SharePoint permissions.** Review Site Owner and Site Collection Administrator assignments. Apply the principle of least privilege rigorously and remove unnecessary elevated access.\n- **Inspect for indicators of compromise.** Review SharePoint server logs for unusual authentication patterns, unexpected file uploads (particularly .aspx web shells), anomalous process execution, and outbound network connections from SharePoint servers.\n\n**Ongoing:**\n- **Implement network segmentation.** SharePoint servers should not have unrestricted outbound internet access. Segment them from critical assets and monitor east-west traffic for lateral movement indicators.\n- **Deploy endpoint detection and response (EDR)** on SharePoint servers to detect post-exploitation activity such as process injection, credential dumping, or persistence mechanisms.\n- **Enable enhanced logging.** Ensure SharePoint ULS logs, Windows Security Event logs, and IIS logs are being collected and forwarded to a SIEM for monitoring and correlation.\n- **Monitor CISA's KEV catalog** as a standing input to your vulnerability management prioritization process. Vulnerabilities listed in the KEV represent confirmed, active threats — not theoretical risks.\n- **Consider migration timelines.** Organizations still running legacy SharePoint Server versions approaching end-of-support should accelerate planning for migration to SharePoint Online or supported on-premises versions.\n\n---\n\n## Industry Response\n\nThe security community's response has been swift. Multiple threat intelligence firms have issued advisories and detection guidance, and several vendors have published Sigma rules, YARA signatures, and Snort rules to help defenders identify exploitation attempts and post-compromise activity.\n\nMicrosoft has reiterated the importance of timely patch application and has made the January cumulative update available through standard Windows Server Update Services (WSUS), Microsoft Update Catalog, and Configuration Manager channels. The company's Security Response Center continues to monitor exploitation activity and has committed to updating its advisory if the threat landscape evolves.\n\nCISA's inclusion of the vulnerability in the KEV catalog also serves a broader strategic purpose: it reinforces the agency's ongoing effort to shift the burden of cybersecurity from end users to technology vendors and to establish clear, enforceable standards for vulnerability remediation across the federal enterprise. The KEV catalog has become one of the most impactful tools in CISA's arsenal, providing a curated, evidence-based list that cuts through the noise of thousands of annual CVE publications to highlight the vulnerabilities that actually matter — because they are actually being exploited.\n\nFor security teams already stretched thin by alert fatigue and competing priorities, the message from CISA is unambiguous: this is not a theoretical risk. Patch your SharePoint servers today.\n\n---\n\n**",
