    "slug": "new-perseus-android-malware-checks-user-notes-for-secrets",
    "headline": "New ‘Perseus’ Android malware checks user notes for secrets",
    "summary": "A new Android malware called Perseus is checking user-curated notes to steal sensitive information, like passwords, recovery phrases, or financial data. [...]",
    "body": "# New 'Perseus' Android Malware Harvests Sensitive Data From User Notes Apps\n\n## A Novel Approach to Credential Theft Targets the Human Habit of Storing Secrets in Plain Text\n\nA newly discovered Android malware strain dubbed \"Perseus\" is exploiting one of the most common — and most warned-against — user behaviors in mobile security: storing passwords, cryptocurrency recovery phrases, and financial credentials in notes applications. First identified by mobile threat researchers in early 2026, Perseus represents a targeted evolution in Android infostealers, moving beyond traditional overlay attacks and keylogging to mine the troves of sensitive data users unwittingly curate for themselves.\n\n---\n\n## Background and Context\n\nFor years, security professionals have cautioned users against treating notes apps as password managers. Despite widespread availability of dedicated credential vaults and password managers, a significant portion of mobile users continue to store sensitive information — Wi-Fi passwords, banking PINs, seed phrases, insurance policy numbers, and even Social Security numbers — in default or third-party notes applications. Perseus operationalizes this well-known behavioral weakness at scale.\n\nThe malware was first flagged by mobile security analysts who observed a spike in data exfiltration traffic from compromised Android devices across Southeast Asia and Latin America in late February 2026. Upon closer analysis, researchers determined the exfiltrated data did not originate from banking overlays or accessibility service abuse in the traditional sense. Instead, the malware was methodically reading, parsing, and exfiltrating the contents of notes applications — including Google Keep, Samsung Notes, OneNote, and several popular third-party alternatives.\n\nWhat makes Perseus particularly concerning is its patience. Unlike smash-and-grab infostealers that immediately attempt to harvest credentials from banking apps, Perseus operates quietly, indexing note contents over time and applying keyword-based filtering to identify high-value entries before exfiltrating them to command-and-control infrastructure.\n\n---\n\n## Technical Details\n\nPerseus is distributed primarily through trojanized utility applications — QR code scanners, PDF converters, and file managers — hosted on third-party app stores and promoted through malicious ad campaigns. Some samples have also been observed leveraging phishing links delivered via SMS and messaging platforms such as WhatsApp and Telegram.\n\nUpon installation, the malware requests standard permissions that appear innocuous for its disguise category, including storage access. On Android versions prior to 13, broad storage permissions grant sufficient access to read local note databases. On newer Android versions with scoped storage restrictions, Perseus leverages the Accessibility Service — a well-documented attack vector — to read on-screen content from notes apps directly.\n\nThe malware's core module operates in several stages:\n\n1. **Enumeration**: Perseus scans for installed notes applications by querying the package manager for known package names. Researchers have identified over 30 targeted apps in the current configuration, suggesting active maintenance by the threat actors.\n\n2. **Content Extraction**: For apps storing data in local SQLite databases (common in many notes apps), Perseus reads entries directly from the database files. For cloud-synced apps, it uses accessibility services to scrape displayed content when the user opens the application.\n\n3. **Keyword Filtering**: Extracted text is run through a local keyword engine that searches for high-value indicators — terms like \"password,\" \"seed,\" \"recovery,\" \"PIN,\" \"wallet,\" \"private key,\" \"login,\" and their equivalents in multiple languages including Spanish, Portuguese, Mandarin, and Hindi. This multilingual capability suggests a globally-minded operation.\n\n4. **Staged Exfiltration**: Matching entries are encrypted using a hardcoded RSA public key and exfiltrated to C2 servers over HTTPS, blended with legitimate-looking API traffic to evade network-level detection. Exfiltration occurs only when the device is connected to Wi-Fi and charging — a technique designed to avoid arousing user suspicion through unexpected battery drain or data consumption.\n\nResearchers also noted that Perseus includes a secondary module capable of capturing screenshots when cryptocurrency wallet applications are in the foreground, indicating a particular interest in digital asset theft.\n\n---\n\n## Real-World Impact\n\nThe implications are substantial. Cryptocurrency recovery phrases stored in notes apps provide irrevocable access to digital wallets. Unlike banking credentials, which can be reset or frozen, a compromised 12- or 24-word seed phrase gives an attacker permanent, irreversible control over associated funds. Early victim reports suggest losses ranging from modest sums to six-figure cryptocurrency holdings.\n\nBeyond financial theft, the exposure of passwords stored in notes creates cascading risk. A single compromised note containing a user's email password, for instance, can serve as a pivot point for account takeover across dozens of services. For enterprise environments, employees who store work credentials in personal notes apps represent an unquantified shadow IT risk that traditional endpoint detection may not address.\n\nOrganizations with BYOD policies face particular exposure. A compromised personal device carrying corporate credentials in a notes app falls outside the visibility of most mobile device management solutions, especially when the credentials are stored as unstructured text rather than within a managed application.\n\n---\n\n## Threat Actor Context\n\nAttribution remains preliminary. The C2 infrastructure associated with Perseus samples has been traced to bulletproof hosting providers with historical ties to financially motivated cybercrime groups operating out of Eastern Europe, though researchers caution that infrastructure alone is insufficient for confident attribution.\n\nThe multilingual keyword engine and geographic distribution of early victims — concentrated in Southeast Asia, Latin America, and parts of Southern Europe — suggest a threat actor with broad financial ambitions rather than nation-state espionage objectives. The operational tradecraft, including anti-analysis techniques such as emulator detection, delayed execution, and geographic fencing, reflects a moderately sophisticated operation consistent with organized cybercrime rather than an amateur campaign.\n\nSome analysts have noted structural similarities between Perseus and the Anatsa (TeaBot) banking trojan family, though no definitive code-level connection has been established. It remains possible that Perseus is the work of a former affiliate or a group that has studied established Android malware families closely.\n\n---\n\n## Defensive Recommendations\n\nSecurity professionals and end users should consider the following mitigations:\n\n- **Audit notes applications immediately.** Search for and remove any stored passwords, seed phrases, PINs, or sensitive credentials. Migrate them to a dedicated password manager with encrypted storage.\n- **Restrict sideloading.** Disable installation from unknown sources on all managed devices. Perseus distribution relies heavily on third-party app stores.\n- **Monitor Accessibility Service grants.** Audit which applications have been granted accessibility permissions. No utility app — especially a QR scanner or file manager — should require this permission.\n- **Enforce app vetting in BYOD environments.** Organizations should implement mobile threat defense (MTD) solutions capable of detecting trojanized applications and anomalous accessibility service usage.\n- **Educate users.** The core vulnerability Perseus exploits is behavioral. Security awareness training should specifically address the risk of storing credentials in unencrypted notes.\n- **Update to the latest Android version.** Scoped storage restrictions in Android 13 and later reduce — though do not eliminate — the attack surface for direct database access.\n\n---\n\n## Industry Response\n\nGoogle has stated that Google Play Protect detects known Perseus samples and that none of the identified trojanized apps were distributed through the official Play Store. The company reiterated its recommendation that users install applications only from trusted sources and keep Play Protect enabled.\n\nMobile security vendors including Lookout, Zimperium, and ESET have published detection signatures and IOCs for known Perseus variants. The Mobile Security Working Group within the App Defense Alliance has flagged the keyword-scraping technique as an emerging pattern likely to be adopted by other malware families, urging notes app developers to consider implementing encrypted-at-rest storage as a default.\n\nThe broader security community has noted that Perseus is less a technical breakthrough than an operational one — it weaponizes a behavioral vulnerability that has been hiding in plain sight. The malware's success hinges not on a zero-day exploit but on the uncomfortable reality that millions of users continue to store their most sensitive secrets in the digital equivalent of a sticky note.\n\n---\n\n**",
