    "summary": "Phil Venables, former CISO of Google Cloud and now a venture partner at Ballistic Ventures, has joined Native’s board of directors. The post Cloud Security Startup Native Exits Stealth With $42 Million in Funding appeared first on SecurityWeek.",
    "body": "I'll write the article based on the available information and my knowledge of the cloud security landscape.\n\n---\n\n# Cloud Security Startup Native Exits Stealth With $42 Million in Funding, Taps Former Google Cloud CISO for Board\n\n## A New Contender Enters the Cloud Security Arena With Serious Backing and Heavyweight Talent\n\n**Native**, a cloud security startup that has been operating in stealth mode, has officially emerged with a substantial $42 million in funding and a marquee board appointment: **Phil Venables**, the former Chief Information Security Officer of Google Cloud and now a venture partner at Ballistic Ventures, has joined the company's board of directors. The announcement signals growing investor confidence that the cloud security market — already one of the most competitive segments in cybersecurity — still has room for innovative newcomers tackling unsolved problems.\n\n---\n\n## Background and Context\n\nThe cloud security market has experienced explosive growth over the past five years, driven by the accelerating migration of enterprise workloads to public, private, and hybrid cloud environments. Yet despite billions of dollars in investment across the sector, organizations continue to struggle with fundamental challenges: misconfigurations, identity sprawl, insufficient runtime visibility, and the growing complexity of securing cloud-native architectures that span multiple providers and thousands of ephemeral workloads.\n\nNative's emergence from stealth with $42 million — a significant sum that suggests the company has likely completed multiple funding rounds while operating quietly — indicates that its backers believe the startup has identified a meaningful gap in the current cloud security ecosystem. The involvement of Ballistic Ventures, a firm founded by cybersecurity industry veterans specifically to back security startups, lends additional credibility to Native's approach.\n\nPhil Venables' decision to join the board is arguably the most telling signal. During his tenure as Google Cloud's CISO, Venables was responsible for the security posture of one of the world's three largest cloud platforms, giving him an unparalleled vantage point on what works — and what doesn't — in cloud security. His transition to Ballistic Ventures and now to Native's board suggests he sees the company addressing a problem he witnessed firsthand at scale.\n\n---\n\n## Technical Landscape and the Problem Space\n\nWhile specific technical details about Native's platform will become clearer as the company makes its product generally available, the startup enters a market that is undergoing a significant architectural shift. The cloud security industry has seen rapid consolidation around Cloud-Native Application Protection Platforms (CNAPPs), which attempt to unify capabilities like Cloud Security Posture Management (CSPM), Cloud Workload Protection (CWP), Cloud Infrastructure Entitlement Management (CIEM), and Infrastructure-as-Code (IaC) scanning into a single platform.\n\nHowever, many security practitioners have voiced frustration that existing CNAPP solutions generate overwhelming volumes of alerts without adequate context, struggle to correlate risks across different cloud layers, and often fail to keep pace with the rapid evolution of cloud-native services. The shift toward serverless computing, containerized microservices, and infrastructure-as-code pipelines has created new attack surfaces that legacy approaches — even those marketed as \"cloud-native\" — often address only superficially.\n\nStartups entering stealth in recent years have increasingly focused on areas such as cloud detection and response (CDR), real-time runtime protection, cloud identity security, and data security posture management (DSPM). Native's positioning within this landscape will be critical to understanding its competitive differentiation against both established players like Wiz, Orca Security, and Palo Alto Networks' Prisma Cloud, as well as other well-funded newcomers.\n\n---\n\n## Real-World Impact for Organizations\n\nFor enterprise security teams, Native's entrance adds another option to an increasingly crowded vendor landscape — but that may be exactly what the market needs. Despite the proliferation of cloud security tools, breach data tells a sobering story: cloud misconfigurations and identity-based attacks remain among the top causes of data breaches, with incidents affecting major organizations continuing at an alarming pace.\n\nAccording to industry analyses, the average enterprise now uses three or more cloud providers and deploys hundreds of distinct cloud services, creating a security surface that is both vast and constantly shifting. Security teams are often understaffed relative to the complexity they face, and the tools they rely on frequently operate in silos, providing partial visibility without the holistic context needed to prioritize the threats that matter most.\n\nA startup entering the market with $42 million and board-level guidance from someone with Venables' experience is well-positioned to address these pain points — provided its technology delivers on what are sure to be ambitious promises. For CISOs and security architects evaluating their cloud security stack, Native represents a company worth monitoring as it begins to publicly articulate its technical approach and differentiation.\n\n---\n\n## Investor and Industry Context\n\nBallistic Ventures has established itself as one of the more discerning cybersecurity-focused venture firms since its founding, with a portfolio that reflects deep domain expertise rather than trend-chasing. The firm was co-founded by Ted Schlein, a legendary figure in security venture capital, and its partners collectively bring decades of operational security experience. Venables' role as a venture partner at the firm — and now as a Native board member — represents a bridge between operational cloud security expertise and early-stage investment.\n\nThe $42 million funding total is notable in the current venture climate. After the exuberance of 2021-2022, cybersecurity venture funding has become more disciplined, with investors demanding clearer paths to revenue and differentiation. A stealth-mode company raising this amount suggests that Native has likely demonstrated meaningful technical progress — and possibly early customer traction — to sophisticated investors who understand the cloud security market intimately.\n\nThe timing of Native's exit from stealth also coincides with a broader industry trend: the recognition that cloud security must evolve beyond periodic scanning and static analysis toward continuous, context-aware protection that operates at the speed of cloud-native development. This shift has created openings for startups willing to rethink foundational assumptions about how cloud environments should be secured.\n\n---\n\n## Defensive Recommendations\n\nWhile the specifics of Native's offering are still emerging, security leaders should use this moment to reassess their cloud security posture more broadly:\n\n- **Audit your current coverage gaps.** Map your cloud security tools against the MITRE ATT&CK Cloud Matrix to identify where detection and prevention capabilities are thin or absent.\n- **Prioritize identity security.** Cloud breaches increasingly begin with compromised or overprivileged identities. Ensure your CIEM capabilities are robust and continuously monitored.\n- **Evaluate runtime protection.** Static scanning of configurations and IaC templates is necessary but insufficient. Assess whether you have adequate visibility into runtime behavior across containers, serverless functions, and managed services.\n- **Consolidate where possible.** Tool sprawl creates its own security risks through integration gaps and alert fatigue. Consider whether emerging platforms can replace multiple point solutions.\n- **Watch the stealth-mode pipeline.** Native is one of several well-funded startups emerging in the cloud security space. Maintain awareness of new entrants whose approaches may address gaps that incumbent vendors have not resolved.\n\n---\n\n## Industry Response and What's Ahead\n\nThe security community's response to Native's announcement will largely depend on what the company reveals in the coming weeks and months about its technical approach. The cloud security market has seen no shortage of bold claims from well-funded startups, and practitioners have grown appropriately skeptical of pitches that promise to solve everything.\n\nThat said, the caliber of talent and investment behind Native sets a high floor for expectations. Phil Venables is not a figurehead appointment — he is one of the most respected operational security leaders in the industry, and his involvement suggests genuine technical substance behind the company's stealth work.\n\nAs Native begins its public journey, the cloud security market continues to mature and consolidate. The companies that will ultimately succeed are those that deliver measurable security outcomes — fewer breaches, faster detection, more efficient operations — rather than incremental feature additions to an already crowded category. With $42 million in funding and a board that includes one of cloud security's most experienced practitioners, Native has the resources and the guidance to compete. Now comes the hard part: proving it in production.\n\n---\n\n**",
    "tldr": "** Cloud security startup Native exits stealth with $42M in funding and former Google Cloud CISO Phil Venables joining its board, signaling serious investment in next-gen cloud protection.",
