    "body": "# New \"Darksword\" iOS Exploit Kit Leveraged in Targeted Infostealer Campaign Against iPhone Users\n\n## Lead\n\nA sophisticated exploit kit targeting Apple's iOS ecosystem has emerged as one of the more concerning mobile threats of 2026. Dubbed \"Darksword\" by researchers who first identified it in the wild, the framework combines a zero-click exploitation chain with a modular infostealer payload capable of exfiltrating cryptocurrency wallet credentials, messaging app data, and a broad range of personal information from compromised iPhones. The campaign, which appears to have been active since at least early February, has prompted urgent advisories from multiple threat intelligence firms and renewed scrutiny of iOS security assumptions.\n\n## Background and Context\n\nThe Darksword exploit kit was first flagged by mobile threat researchers after a cluster of anomalous data exfiltration events were traced back to iPhones running iOS 17 and iOS 18 builds. Unlike many iOS-targeting campaigns that rely on social engineering or malicious profiles, Darksword employs a multi-stage exploitation chain that requires minimal user interaction — a hallmark of advanced threat tooling historically associated with commercial spyware vendors and nation-state operators.\n\nWhat makes Darksword particularly noteworthy is the breadth of its data collection capabilities. While previous iOS infostealers have typically focused on narrow objectives — such as harvesting iMessage content or GPS location — Darksword's payload is designed to systematically sweep a compromised device for cryptocurrency wallet seed phrases and private keys, authentication tokens from banking and financial applications, saved credentials from the iOS keychain, messaging history across multiple platforms, and photo library metadata and selected images.\n\nThe targeting of cryptocurrency assets marks an evolution in iOS-focused threats. As mobile devices increasingly serve as primary interfaces for managing digital assets, threat actors have shifted their focus accordingly. The inclusion of wallet-draining capabilities alongside traditional infostealer functions suggests an operator motivated by direct financial gain, though the sophistication of the delivery mechanism raises questions about who developed the underlying tooling.\n\n## Technical Details\n\nDarksword's exploitation chain targets a previously undisclosed vulnerability in WebKit, Apple's browser engine that underpins Safari and all third-party browsers on iOS. The initial vector leverages a type confusion flaw in WebKit's JavaScript engine to achieve arbitrary code execution within the browser sandbox. From there, a second-stage exploit targets a kernel vulnerability to escape the sandbox and gain elevated privileges on the device.\n\nThe delivery framework is notably flexible. Researchers have observed Darksword payloads being served through compromised advertising networks — a technique known as malvertising — as well as through carefully crafted phishing pages that mimic legitimate cryptocurrency exchange login portals. In some cases, the exploit chain triggers without any visible interaction from the user beyond loading a webpage, qualifying it as a zero-click or low-interaction attack depending on the delivery path.\n\nOnce the kernel exploit achieves root-level access, the Darksword implant deploys as a persistent process that survives app termination but does not currently appear to persist across device reboots. The implant communicates with command-and-control infrastructure over encrypted channels, using domain-fronting techniques to blend its traffic with legitimate cloud service requests. This makes network-level detection significantly more challenging.\n\nThe infostealer module employs a modular plugin architecture. Each plugin targets a specific category of data — one scans for cryptocurrency wallet applications and attempts to extract seed phrases from app storage, another harvests