    "summary": "Credential theft soared in the second half of 2025, thanks in part to the industrialization of infostealer malware and AI-enabled social engineering.",
    "body": "# More Attackers Are Logging In, Not Breaking In\n\n## The Era of the Stolen Credential\n\nThe perimeter is no longer being breached — it is being bypassed entirely. Data from multiple threat intelligence firms tracking the second half of 2025 confirms a stark shift in attacker methodology: credential theft has overtaken traditional exploitation as the primary initial access vector across enterprise environments. Fueled by the industrialization of infostealer malware and the rapid maturation of AI-enabled social engineering, adversaries are increasingly choosing the path of least resistance — logging in with legitimate credentials rather than breaking through hardened defenses.\n\nThe implications are profound. Organizations that have spent years fortifying their perimeters, patching vulnerabilities, and deploying next-generation firewalls now face a threat model where the attacker looks indistinguishable from an authorized user at the point of entry.\n\n## Background and Context\n\nThe trend did not materialize overnight. Credential-based attacks have been climbing steadily since the pandemic-era explosion of remote work and cloud adoption expanded the attack surface. But the second half of 2025 marked an inflection point. According to threat intelligence reports from IBM X-Force, CrowdStrike, and Flashpoint, valid credential abuse accounted for the largest share of initial access incidents across sectors — surpassing phishing, vulnerability exploitation, and supply chain compromise.\n\nSeveral converging factors drove this acceleration. First, the infostealer malware ecosystem underwent a dramatic industrialization. What was once a fragmented landscape of individually operated trojans has consolidated into a mature, service-oriented underground economy. Malware-as-a-service (MaaS) platforms now offer turnkey infostealer deployments with polished dashboards, automated log parsing, and direct integration with credential marketplaces. Families like Lumma, Raccoon v2, Vidar, and the resurgent RedLine have become commodity tools, accessible to low-sophistication actors for subscription fees as low as $200 per month.\n\nSecond, generative AI has supercharged social engineering at scale. Threat actors are leveraging large language models to craft convincing phishing lures, generate deepfake voice and video for vishing attacks, and automate pretexting conversations that defeat human intuition. The barrier to producing linguistically flawless, contextually targeted social engineering content has effectively collapsed, enabling campaigns that were previously the exclusive domain of well-resourced nation-state groups.\n\n## Technical Details\n\nThe modern infostealer kill chain is alarmingly efficient. A typical infection begins with a user clicking a poisoned search engine result, downloading a trojanized software crack, or opening a malicious attachment delivered via social engineering. Upon execution, the stealer operates in seconds — harvesting browser-stored passwords, session cookies, autofill data, cryptocurrency wallet files, and authentication tokens before exfiltrating the data to a command-and-control server.\n\nWhat makes the current generation of infostealers particularly dangerous is their focus on session tokens and authentication cookies rather than just static passwords. By stealing active session cookies for services like Microsoft 365, Google Workspace, Okta, and corporate VPNs, attackers can bypass multi-factor authentication entirely. The stolen session is already post-authentication — no second factor is required. This technique, known as session hijacking or pass-the-cookie, has rendered traditional MFA implementations far less effective than organizations assume.\n\nThe stolen credentials and session data flow into a sophisticated underground supply chain. Initial access brokers (IABs) aggregate, validate, and categorize stolen credentials before listing them on dark web marketplaces and Telegram channels. Pricing follows market logic: a set of credentials for a Fortune 500 VPN portal commands a premium, while bulk consumer credentials sell for fractions of a cent. Automated bots perform credential stuffing and validation at scale, ensuring that buyers receive working access rather than stale data.\n\nAI augmentation extends beyond social engineering into the credential exploitation pipeline itself. Threat actors are using machine learning models to correlate stolen credential datasets, predict password reuse patterns, and prioritize high-value targets. Automated systems can now take a freshly harvested credential log, identify corporate email addresses, cross-reference them with exposed infrastructure, and generate prioritized target lists — all without human intervention.\n\n## Real-World Impact\n\nThe downstream consequences are severe and wide-ranging. Once inside a network with valid credentials, an attacker inherits the trust level of the compromised account. Security tools designed to flag anomalous exploitation behavior — unusual process execution, lateral movement via exploit frameworks, privilege escalation through known CVEs — often fail to trigger on what appears to be normal user activity.\n\nThis has fundamentally altered incident response timelines. Dwell times for credential-based intrusions are significantly longer than those involving malware or exploit chains, precisely because the initial access leaves fewer forensic artifacts. In multiple high-profile breaches disclosed in late 2025, attackers maintained persistent access for weeks or months using nothing more than stolen credentials and living-off-the-land techniques.\n\nThe financial sector, healthcare, and technology companies have been disproportionately affected, though no industry is immune. Business email compromise (BEC) schemes powered by stolen credentials continue to generate billions in losses annually. Ransomware operators increasingly purchase initial access from IABs rather than developing their own exploitation capabilities, compressing the timeline from credential theft to full environment compromise.\n\n## Threat Actor Context\n\nThe democratization of credential theft has blurred traditional threat actor boundaries. Nation-state groups, including clusters tracked as APT29, APT41, and Midnight Blizzard, have been observed purchasing credentials from criminal marketplaces to supplement their own collection operations. Meanwhile, financially motivated groups like Scattered Spider have built entire operational playbooks around social engineering and credential abuse, demonstrating that sophisticated intrusions no longer require sophisticated exploitation.\n\nThe infostealer ecosystem itself is dominated by Russian-speaking and CIS-based cybercriminal groups, though the customer base is global. The commoditization of access has created a layered criminal economy where malware developers, log aggregators, initial access brokers, and end-stage operators function as distinct, specialized entities — each optimizing their segment of the kill chain.\n\n## Defensive Recommendations\n\nOrganizations must adapt their defensive posture to address the credential threat head-on:\n\n- **Implement phishing-resistant MFA.** FIDO2/WebAuthn hardware security keys and passkeys are resistant to session hijacking and adversary-in-the-middle attacks. Traditional SMS and TOTP-based MFA should be treated as insufficient against current threats.\n- **Deploy continuous session validation.** Implement token binding, device trust verification, and conditional access policies that re-evaluate sessions based on behavioral signals such as impossible travel, device fingerprint changes, and anomalous access patterns.\n- **Adopt credential exposure monitoring.** Subscribe to threat intelligence feeds that monitor dark web marketplaces, paste sites, and Telegram channels for organizational credential exposure. Automate password resets when exposure is detected.\n- **Harden endpoint browsers.** Enforce enterprise browser policies that disable password storage in browsers, mandate password manager usage, and restrict extension installations. Consider browser isolation for high-risk activities.\n- **Invest in identity threat detection and response (ITDR).** Traditional EDR and SIEM are necessary but insufficient. ITDR platforms specifically monitor for credential misuse patterns including impossible concurrent sessions, privilege escalation anomalies, and authentication graph deviations.\n- **Conduct infostealer-specific tabletop exercises.** Simulate a scenario where an employee's home device is compromised by an infostealer and corporate credentials are exfiltrated. Test whether detection, response, and recovery processes are adequate.\n\n## Industry Response\n\nThe security community is responding with urgency, though the structural challenge remains daunting. Major identity providers are accelerating passkey adoption — Microsoft, Google, and Apple have all expanded passkey support across their ecosystems throughout 2025. The FIDO Alliance has pushed new guidelines for enterprise passkey deployment, and several large organizations have begun mandatory migration timelines.\n\nThreat intelligence firms have expanded their credential monitoring capabilities, with platforms like Flare, SpyCloud, and Hudson Rock building automated remediation workflows that integrate directly with identity providers to force credential rotation upon detection of exposure.\n\nOn the regulatory front, the SEC's updated cybersecurity disclosure rules and the EU's NIS2 directive are pressuring organizations to demonstrate adequate identity security controls, creating compliance incentives that align with security best practices.\n\nYet the fundamental asymmetry persists. Defenders must protect every credential across every user and every service. Attackers need only one. Until the industry completes the transition away from passwords and phishable authentication factors — a migration that remains years from completion — the advantage will continue to favor those who log in over those who break in.\n\n---\n\n**",
