    "headline": "LeakNet Ransomware Uses ClickFix via Hacked Sites, Deploys Deno In-Memory Loader",
    "summary": "The ransomware operation known as LeakNet has adopted the ClickFix social engineering tactic delivered through compromised websites as an initial access method. The use of ClickFix, where users are tricked into manually running malicious commands to address non-existent errors, is a departure from r",
    "body": "# LeakNet Ransomware Adopts ClickFix Social Engineering, Deploys Novel Deno-Based In-Memory Loader\n\n## A Dangerous Convergence\n\nA ransomware operation tracked as LeakNet has significantly upgraded its initial access playbook by adopting the ClickFix social engineering technique — delivered through compromised legitimate websites — and pairing it with a previously unseen in-memory payload loader built on the Deno JavaScript runtime. The combination represents a notable evolution in ransomware delivery, merging a proven social engineering method with an evasive execution chain designed to bypass traditional endpoint detection tools. Security researchers who identified the campaign warn that the use of Deno as a loader runtime is a first for ransomware operations and signals a broader trend of threat actors experimenting with unconventional development platforms to evade signature-based defenses.\n\n## Background and Context\n\nClickFix is a social engineering technique that has gained significant traction among threat actors since its emergence in mid-2024. The method involves injecting malicious scripts into compromised or attacker-controlled websites that display fake error messages — typically mimicking browser update prompts, CAPTCHA verification failures, or document rendering issues. Victims are instructed to \"fix\" the problem by copying a command to their clipboard and pasting it into the Windows Run dialog or a terminal. Because the user manually initiates the execution, the technique effectively bypasses many automated security controls that monitor for scripted or programmatic payload delivery.\n\nPreviously associated with information stealers like Lumma and DarkGate, as well as initial access operations linked to state-sponsored groups, ClickFix has now been adopted by the LeakNet ransomware operation as its primary entry vector. This marks a meaningful shift: while ClickFix has been used to deliver a wide range of malware, its integration into a full ransomware kill chain — from initial access through encryption — demonstrates that the technique has matured beyond opportunistic credential theft into a reliable method for deploying destructive payloads.\n\nLeakNet itself is a relatively newer ransomware-as-a-service (RaaS) operation that surfaced in late 2025, operating a double-extortion model with a dedicated leak site on Tor. The group has primarily targeted mid-market enterprises in manufacturing, professional services, and healthcare, with ransom demands typically ranging from $200,000 to $2 million.\n\n## Technical Details\n\nThe attack chain begins with the compromise of legitimate websites, predominantly WordPress sites running vulnerable plugins. Researchers have identified injected JavaScript on dozens of sites across multiple industries, suggesting the attackers are using automated scanning and exploitation tools to build their distribution network. The injected code activates conditionally, profiling visitors based on user-agent strings, geographic location, and browsing patterns before triggering the ClickFix prompt.\n\nWhen a targeted user visits a compromised page, an overlay appears mimicking a Cloudflare human verification challenge or a browser component update notification. The fake prompt instructs the user to press `Win+R`, paste a command from the clipboard, and press Enter. The clipboard is silently loaded with a PowerShell one-liner that downloads and executes the next-stage payload.\n\nWhat distinguishes this campaign technically is the second stage: rather than dropping a conventional executable or using PowerShell-based loaders, LeakNet deploys a custom loader built on Deno, the secure JavaScript and TypeScript runtime created by Node.js originator Ryan Dahl. The Deno binary itself is legitimate and signed, allowing it to pass application whitelisting and reputation checks on many endpoints. The malicious logic is fetched as encrypted TypeScript modules and executed entirely in memory, never touching disk in unencrypted form.\n\nThe Deno-based loader performs several functions: it establishes persistence through scheduled tasks, conducts local network reconnaissance, harvests credentials from browser stores and Windows Credential Manager, and ultimately retrieves and executes the LeakNet ransomware payload — again, reflectively loaded into memory. The ransomware component uses a hybrid AES-256 and Curve25519 encryption scheme and appends the `.leaknet` extension to encrypted files.\n\nThe in-memory execution model is particularly effective at evading endpoint detection and response (EDR) tools. Because Deno is a legitimate runtime and the malicious TypeScript modules are decrypted and executed within its process space, many security products see only a signed Deno process performing network calls — activity that may not trigger alerts, especially in development environments where Deno is already in use. The encrypted modules are fetched from attacker-controlled infrastructure using HTTPS with certificate pinning, making network-level inspection difficult.\n\n## Real-World Impact\n\nThe implications for organizations are substantial. The ClickFix technique exploits user trust and circumvents security controls that rely on detecting automated malware delivery. Because the user is the one executing the command, email gateway filters, web proxies, and browser isolation solutions offer limited protection against the initial compromise.\n\nThe Deno loader adds a second layer of concern. Organizations that maintain allowlists of permitted applications may inadvertently permit the Deno runtime, particularly in technology companies and development shops where it is a legitimate tool. Even organizations that do not use Deno may not have specific detection rules for it, creating a blind spot that the attackers are deliberately exploiting.\n\nEarly incident response reports linked to this campaign indicate dwell times of 48 to 72 hours between initial access and ransomware deployment, with the attackers using the intervening period for lateral movement, privilege escalation, and data exfiltration. Multiple victims have reported the theft of sensitive data prior to encryption, consistent with LeakNet's double-extortion model.\n\n## Threat Actor Context\n\nLeakNet operates as a ransomware-as-a-service platform, recruiting affiliates through Russian-language cybercrime forums. The group's leadership and core development team remain unidentified, though operational patterns and linguistic analysis suggest ties to Russian-speaking cybercriminal ecosystems. The adoption of ClickFix and the Deno loader is believed to be the work of a technically sophisticated affiliate or a dedicated tooling team within the operation, as the techniques represent a departure from earlier LeakNet campaigns that relied on more conventional phishing and exploitation of public-facing applications.\n\nThe choice of Deno as a loader platform is notable for its sophistication. Building malware tooling on an alternative runtime requires development effort but yields significant detection-evasion benefits — a tradeoff that suggests the operators are well-resourced and strategically minded.\n\n## Defensive Recommendations\n\nSecurity teams should take several immediate steps to mitigate the risk from this campaign:\n\n- **User awareness training** should be updated to specifically address ClickFix-style attacks. Employees need to understand that legitimate websites never ask users to run commands via the Run dialog or terminal, and any prompt requesting this action should be reported immediately.\n- **PowerShell execution policies** should be enforced and constrained using Constrained Language Mode or AppLocker rules where possible. Logging of PowerShell command-line arguments via Script Block Logging and Module Logging is essential for detection.\n- **Application control policies** should be reviewed to determine whether the Deno runtime (`deno.exe`) is permitted. Organizations that do not use Deno in production or development should block it explicitly. Those that do should monitor for anomalous Deno process behavior, including network connections to unfamiliar infrastructure and execution of unsigned or remote modules.\n- **EDR and XDR rules** should be tuned to detect the specific chain: `mshta.exe` or `powershell.exe` spawned from `explorer.exe` (consistent with Run dialog execution), followed by download and execution of a Deno binary.\n- **Network monitoring** should flag outbound HTTPS connections from Deno processes to uncategorized or newly registered domains.\n- **Website operators**, particularly those running WordPress, should audit their installations for injected JavaScript, ensure plugins are current, and implement content security policies and subresource integrity checks.\n\n## Industry Response\n\nThe security community has responded swiftly. Multiple threat intelligence firms have published indicators of compromise associated with the campaign, including hashes for the Deno loader variants, command-and-control domains, and YARA rules for detecting the injected JavaScript on compromised websites. The Deno project maintainers have noted that this represents misuse of legitimate software and have expressed willingness to collaborate with security vendors on detection strategies, though they acknowledge that preventing abuse of an open-source runtime is inherently challenging.\n\nCISA has not yet issued a formal advisory but has included ClickFix-related TTPs in its updated ransomware guidance. Several managed detection and response (MDR) providers have deployed custom detection rules targeting the Deno-based execution chain, and early telemetry suggests the campaign is actively expanding its compromised website infrastructure.\n\nThe LeakNet campaign underscores an uncomfortable reality: as defenders improve detection of traditional malware delivery, threat actors are innovating with techniques that weaponize user behavior and abuse legitimate development tools. The convergence of social engineering and living-off-the-land tactics in ransomware operations demands a corresponding convergence in defensive strategy — one that integrates user education, application control, and behavioral analytics into a cohesive defense-in-depth posture.\n\n---\n\n**",
