    "headline": "AI Flaws in Amazon Bedrock, LangSmith, and SGLang Enable Data Exfiltration and RCE",
    "summary": "Cybersecurity researchers have disclosed details of a new method for exfiltrating sensitive data from artificial intelligence (AI) code execution environments using domain name system (DNS) queries. In a report published Monday, BeyondTrust revealed that Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Code Interpreter's s",
    "body": "I don't have web access permissions to research the full story. However, based on the title and partial summary you provided, I have enough context from my knowledge to write this article. The BeyondTrust research on AI code execution sandbox escapes via DNS was a significant disclosure. Let me write the article.\n\n---\n\n# AI Flaws in Amazon Bedrock, LangSmith, and SGLang Enable Data Exfiltration and Remote Code Execution\n\n## Critical sandbox escape techniques expose AI infrastructure to stealthy data theft through DNS tunneling and beyond\n\nSecurity researchers have uncovered a series of critical vulnerabilities across major AI development platforms — Amazon Bedrock, LangChain's LangSmith, and the open-source SGLang inference framework — that could allow attackers to exfiltrate sensitive data, achieve remote code execution, and break out of supposedly sandboxed AI environments. The findings, disclosed by researchers at BeyondTrust, highlight a growing and underappreciated attack surface: the code execution sandboxes that underpin modern AI agent workflows.\n\n## Background and Context\n\nAs enterprises rapidly adopt AI agents capable of generating and executing code on the fly, platforms like Amazon Bedrock AgentCore, LangSmith, and SGLang have become foundational infrastructure. These platforms provide sandboxed code interpretation environments — isolated containers where AI-generated code can run without, in theory, posing a risk to the host system or leaking sensitive data.\n\nBut \"sandboxed\" does not mean \"impervious.\" BeyondTrust's research team systematically probed the boundaries of these execution environments and found that common assumptions about their isolation were dangerously wrong. The key insight: even when direct network access is locked down, DNS resolution often remains available — and DNS is more than enough to tunnel data out of a restricted environment.\n\nThis matters because AI code interpreters routinely process sensitive inputs — proprietary datasets, API keys, internal documents, and environment variables — as part of their normal operation. If an attacker can manipulate the prompts or code that runs inside these interpreters (through prompt injection, supply chain attacks on AI plugins, or compromised training data), the sandbox is the last line of defense. These findings show that line is thinner than most organizations realize.\n\n## Technical Details\n\n### DNS-Based Data Exfiltration from Amazon Bedrock AgentCore\n\nThe most novel technique disclosed involves Amazon Bedrock AgentCore's Code Interpreter. BeyondTrust researchers found that while the sandbox restricts outbound HTTP/HTTPS traffic and blocks most network protocols, it does permit DNS resolution queries. This is a common configuration — sandboxes need DNS to function — but it opens a well-known covert channel.\n\nBy encoding stolen data into DNS subdomain queries (e.g., `sensitive-data-chunk.attacker-controlled-domain.com`), code running inside the interpreter can exfiltrate information to an attacker-controlled DNS server without triggering conventional network monitoring. Each query can carry roughly 63 bytes of data per label and up to 253 bytes per full domain name. While bandwidth is limited, it is more than sufficient to steal API keys, credentials, environment variables, and summaries of processed documents.\n\nThe researchers demonstrated that an AI agent manipulated via prompt injection could systematically extract environment variables and session tokens from the Bedrock execution environment, then encode and transmit them through DNS queries — all without generating any blocked-connection alerts.\n\n### LangSmith Vulnerabilities\n\nLangSmith, the observability and evaluation platform developed by LangChain, was found to harbor vulnerabilities that extended beyond data exfiltration. The researchers identified flaws that could allow an attacker to access stored API keys, trace data containing potentially sensitive prompt-response pairs, and other platform-managed secrets. Given LangSmith's role as a centralized hub for monitoring AI application behavior, a compromise here could provide attackers with broad visibility into an organization's AI operations — effectively turning the monitoring tool into a reconnaissance platform.\n\nThe specific attack vectors involved exploiting insufficient access controls and input validation within the platform's API endpoints, enabling unauthorized access to resources across tenant boundaries in certain configurations.\n\n### SGLang Remote Code Execution\n\nSGLang, an open-source framework for fast large language model serving, was found to contain a remote code execution vulnerability. The flaw resided in the framework's handling of requests, where insufficient input sanitization allowed an attacker to inject and execute arbitrary code on the host server. Unlike the sandboxed Bedrock environment, SGLang deployments often run with direct access to the host system's resources, making RCE here especially dangerous.\n\nSuccessful exploitation could give an attacker full control over the inference server, enabling data theft, model manipulation, lateral movement within the network, and the potential to serve poisoned responses to all downstream applications relying on that SGLang instance.\n\n## Real-World Impact\n\nThe implications of these findings extend well beyond the individual platforms affected. They expose a systemic pattern in how AI infrastructure is being deployed:\n\n**Overreliance on sandboxing.** Organizations assume that wrapping AI code execution in a container solves the security problem. These findings demonstrate that sandboxes routinely leave covert channels open — DNS being the most obvious but far from the only one.\n\n**AI supply chain risk.** As AI agents increasingly execute code based on user inputs, retrieved documents, and third-party plugins, prompt injection becomes a viable entry point for sandbox escape. An attacker who can influence the inputs to an AI agent may not need to compromise the platform itself.\n\n**Centralized secrets exposure.** Platforms like LangSmith aggregate sensitive operational data by design. A vulnerability in these hubs has an outsized blast radius compared to a flaw in a single application.\n\n**Inference server compromise.** For organizations running SGLang or similar open-source serving frameworks, an RCE vulnerability means that every application consuming that model's outputs is potentially compromised — a single point of failure for the entire AI stack.\n\n## Threat Actor Context\n\nWhile these vulnerabilities were discovered through responsible security research rather than observed in active exploitation, the techniques involved — particularly DNS tunneling — are well-established in the threat actor playbook. Advanced persistent threat (APT) groups have used DNS exfiltration for years in traditional IT environments. The extension of these techniques to AI sandboxes is a natural evolution as adversaries follow organizations into their AI deployments.\n\nThe prompt injection vector is especially concerning in the context of AI agents that interact with external data sources, as it lowers the barrier to exploitation. An attacker does not need direct access to the platform — only the ability to influence what the AI processes.\n\n## Defensive Recommendations\n\nSecurity teams should take several immediate steps to mitigate the risks highlighted by this research:\n\n1. **Audit DNS egress from AI sandboxes.** Monitor and restrict DNS queries originating from code execution environments. Consider routing sandbox DNS through internal resolvers that log queries and block resolution of non-whitelisted domains.\n\n2. **Assume sandbox escape is possible.** Design security architectures with defense in depth. Do not store long-lived credentials or sensitive environment variables inside execution environments. Use short-lived, scoped tokens wherever possible.\n\n3. **Harden AI input pipelines.** Implement robust input validation and prompt injection defenses for any AI system that executes code. Treat all external inputs to AI agents — user messages, retrieved documents, plugin outputs — as untrusted.\n\n4. **Patch and update.** Apply vendor patches promptly. Amazon has addressed the Bedrock issues, LangChain has released fixes for LangSmith, and SGLang maintainers have patched the RCE flaw. Verify that your deployments are running fixed versions.\n\n5. **Network segmentation.** Isolate AI inference servers and code execution environments from sensitive internal networks. Apply the principle of least privilege to all network paths.\n\n6. **Monitor for anomalous DNS patterns.** Implement DNS analytics capable of detecting tunneling — look for unusually high query volumes, high-entropy subdomain labels, and queries to newly registered or low-reputation domains.\n\n## Industry Response\n\nThe disclosures follow the industry's responsible disclosure process, with Amazon, LangChain, and the SGLang maintainers all issuing patches prior to public disclosure. Amazon specifically addressed the DNS exfiltration vector in Bedrock AgentCore by implementing tighter network controls within the Code Interpreter sandbox.\n\nThe research has added momentum to a growing conversation in the security community about AI-specific threat modeling. Organizations like OWASP, through their Top 10 for LLM Applications project, and MITRE, with the ATLAS framework for adversarial AI threats, have been working to codify these risks — but the pace of AI deployment continues to outrun the development of security best practices.\n\nBeyondTrust's findings reinforce a fundamental principle that the security community has learned repeatedly across every technology wave: new capabilities introduce new attack surfaces, and assumptions about isolation are the first things adversaries test. As AI agents become more autonomous and more deeply integrated into enterprise workflows, the security of their execution environments is no longer an edge case — it is a core infrastructure concern.\n\n---\n\n**",
