    "headline": "GlassWorm malware hits 400+ code repos on GitHub, npm, VSCode, OpenVSX",
    "summary": "The GlassWorm supply-chain campaign has returned with a new, coordinated attack that targeted hundreds of packages, repositories, and extensions on GitHub, npm, and VSCode/OpenVSX extensions. [...]",
    "body": "Now I have comprehensive context from multiple related GlassWorm articles. Here's the full rewrite:\n\n---\n\n# GlassWorm Malware Hits 400+ Code Repos on GitHub, npm, VSCode, and OpenVSX\n\n## A Multi-Platform Supply-Chain Assault Targets the Developer Ecosystem at Scale\n\nThe GlassWorm supply-chain campaign has resurfaced with its most ambitious offensive yet — a coordinated multi-platform attack that simultaneously planted malicious code across more than 400 packages, repositories, and extensions spanning GitHub, npm, the Visual Studio Code Marketplace, and the Open VSX Registry. Disclosed by researchers on March 18, 2026, the latest wave marks a dramatic escalation from the campaign's earlier iterations and represents one of the broadest developer-ecosystem compromises observed this year.\n\n---\n\n## Background and Context\n\nGlassWorm first entered the threat landscape in early 2026 as a supply-chain operation focused on poisoning open-source code at its source. Initial attacks were comparatively narrow — dozens of typosquatted npm packages and a handful of rogue VS Code extensions designed to harvest credentials from developers who installed them. But over the span of just a few weeks, the operators behind GlassWorm have demonstrated a capacity for rapid iteration that has alarmed the security research community.\n\nOn March 14, researchers at The Hacker News detailed how GlassWorm had begun abusing 72 Open VSX extensions, calling it a \"significant escalation\" in propagation technique. Rather than embedding malicious loaders directly into every listing, the threat actor started abusing extension dependency chains — a technique that allowed a single compromised dependency to silently infect dozens of otherwise legitimate extensions. By March 16, SecurityWeek reported on what it dubbed \"ForceMemo,\" a secondary campaign in which hundreds of GitHub accounts were accessed using credentials stolen during the earlier VS Code GlassWorm waves, leading to force-pushed malware injections into Python repositories.\n\nThe March 18 disclosure ties these threads together. What initially appeared to be separate campaigns now looks like a single, orchestrated operation spanning at least four major platforms — GitHub, npm, the VS Code Marketplace, and the Open VSX Registry — with a combined footprint exceeding 400 compromised packages, repositories, and extensions.\n\n---\n\n## Technical Details\n\nGlassWorm's technical sophistication lies not in any single novel exploit but in the coordination and layering of well-understood supply-chain attack vectors across multiple ecosystems simultaneously.\n\n**Dependency chain abuse.** In the Open VSX and VS Code Marketplace vectors, GlassWorm operators published seemingly benign utility extensions that were then added as dependencies to more popular listings — either through typosquatting or by contributing to legitimate projects. The malicious payload was buried several layers deep in the dependency tree, making it invisible to casual code review. When the host extension loaded, the dependency chain eventually resolved to a loader that fetched an obfuscated second-stage payload from an attacker-controlled CDN.\n\n**Credential harvesting and token theft.** Early GlassWorm extensions included keyloggers and credential-harvesting modules that targeted developer environments specifically. The stolen GitHub personal access tokens (PATs) and OAuth tokens became the fuel for the next phase: automated force-push attacks against repositories the compromised developers had write access to. According to StepSecurity's analysis, the attack specifically targeted Python projects — Django applications, machine learning research code, Streamlit dashboards, and PyPI packages — appending obfuscated code to common entry points like `setup.py`, `main.py`, and `app.py`.\n\n**npm package poisoning.** On the npm side, GlassWorm employed a mix of typosquatting and dependency confusion. Malicious packages mimicked popular libraries with subtly altered names and included post-install scripts that executed the GlassWorm loader. Some packages were published under accounts that had been compromised via credential reuse from the earlier token-theft phase, lending them an appearance of legitimacy that automated scanners were slow to flag.\n\n**Evasion techniques.** The latest iteration introduced notable evasion improvements. Researchers at Dark Reading reported that newer GlassWorm payloads employ environment-aware execution — checking for the presence of CI/CD runners, sandboxes, and security analysis tools before activating. Payloads that detect a non-developer environment simply remain dormant, reducing the likelihood of detection by automated scanning infrastructure.\n\n---\n\n## Real-World Impact\n\nThe implications for organizations are severe and multi-layered. Any development team that installed a compromised extension, pulled a poisoned npm package, or cloned an infected repository during the campaign window may have exposed their local development credentials to the GlassWorm operators. Because developer workstations typically hold elevated access — SSH keys, cloud provider credentials, CI/CD tokens, and database connection strings — a single compromised developer machine can serve as a beachhead into an organization's entire software delivery pipeline.\n\nThe force-push aspect of the campaign is particularly concerning. Malicious commits injected directly into repository histories can propagate downstream to every developer, CI runner, and deployment pipeline that pulls from those repositories. Organizations that lack signed commits, branch protection rules, or automated code-integrity checks may have unknowingly deployed GlassWorm-tainted code to production environments.\n\nThe breadth of the attack — spanning JavaScript, Python, and IDE extension ecosystems — also means that no single language community or tooling choice provides immunity. The campaign was designed to cast as wide a net as possible across the developer population.\n\n---\n\n## Threat Actor Context\n\nAttribution for GlassWorm remains uncertain. The campaign's operational tempo — escalating from a narrow extension-poisoning effort to a multi-platform, multi-language assault in under two weeks — suggests a well-resourced and organized threat actor, though no government or known APT group has been publicly linked to the operation.\n\nThe profit motive is unclear as well. While credential theft and supply-chain compromise are consistent with both financially motivated cybercrime and espionage operations, the sheer breadth of targeting (rather than focusing on specific high-value organizations) leans toward an opportunistic or access-broker model, where stolen credentials and compromised pipelines may be sold downstream to other threat actors.\n\nSecurity researchers have noted infrastructure overlaps with previous typosquatting campaigns, but no definitive connection has been established. The investigation remains active.\n\n---\n\n## Defensive Recommendations\n\nOrganizations should take immediate action to assess their exposure and harden their developer supply chains:\n\n- **Audit installed VS Code and Open VSX extensions.** Remove any extensions that are unfamiliar, recently updated by unknown publishers, or flagged in published GlassWorm IOC lists. Pay particular attention to extension dependency chains.\n- **Rotate developer credentials.** Any developer who installed a suspicious extension or package should rotate all GitHub tokens, SSH keys, cloud credentials, and CI/CD secrets immediately.\n- **Review repository commit history.** Check for unexpected force-pushed commits, especially in Python projects. Look for obfuscated code appended to `setup.py`, `main.py`, `app.py`, and similar entry points.\n- **Enable branch protection and signed commits.** Require pull request reviews and GPG-signed commits on all production branches to prevent unauthorized force-pushes.\n- **Pin dependencies and use lock files.** Avoid floating version ranges in `package.json` and `requirements.txt`. Use lock files and verify package integrity hashes.\n- **Deploy software composition analysis (SCA).** Tools like Socket, Snyk, or Dependabot can flag suspicious package behavior, typosquatting, and unexpected post-install scripts.\n- **Monitor for anomalous token usage.** GitHub's security log and audit API can reveal token usage from unexpected IP addresses or geographies — a strong signal of compromised PATs.\n\n---\n\n## Industry Response\n\nThe security community has responded with urgency. GitHub has begun notifying affected repository owners and revoking compromised tokens identified through its abuse-detection systems. The npm security team has removed confirmed malicious packages, though the whack-a-mole nature of typosquatting means new variants continue to surface.\n\nMicrosoft and the Open VSX Foundation have pulled flagged extensions from their respective marketplaces and are working with researchers to identify additional compromised listings. StepSecurity, which published one of the earliest technical analyses of the force-push phase, has released open-source tooling to help organizations scan their repositories for GlassWorm indicators of compromise.\n\nThe broader conversation is shifting toward systemic reforms. The GlassWorm campaign has renewed calls for mandatory two-factor authentication on package registries, stricter publisher verification, and ecosystem-wide adoption of package provenance standards like Sigstore. While none of these measures would eliminate supply-chain risk entirely, the consensus among researchers is that the current trust model — where a single compromised account can silently poison hundreds of downstream consumers — is no longer sustainable.\n\nGlassWorm is not the first supply-chain campaign to exploit the open-source ecosystem's trust model, and it will not be the last. But its speed, coordination, and cross-platform reach have set a new benchmark for what defenders must prepare for.\n\n---\n\n**",
