    "headline": "LeakNet ransomware uses ClickFix, Deno runtime in stealthy attacks",
    "summary": "The LeakNet ransomware gang is now using the ClickFix technique for initial access into corporate environments and deploys a malware loader based on the open-source Deno runtime for JavaScript and TypeScript. [...]",
    "body": "# LeakNet Ransomware Adopts ClickFix Social Engineering and Deno-Based Loader in Escalating Campaign\n\nThe LeakNet ransomware operation has significantly upgraded its attack chain, combining the increasingly popular ClickFix social engineering technique with a novel malware loader built on the Deno JavaScript runtime to bypass traditional endpoint defenses and establish persistent footholds in corporate networks. The evolution marks a concerning convergence of two emerging trends in the threat landscape: the weaponization of legitimate developer tools and the refinement of user-interaction-based initial access methods that sidestep conventional email security controls.\n\n## Background and Context\n\nLeakNet, a ransomware-as-a-service (RaaS) operation that first surfaced in late 2025, has historically relied on phishing emails and exploitation of exposed remote access services for initial entry. However, recent incident response engagements analyzed by multiple threat intelligence firms reveal a tactical pivot that aligns the group with a broader trend among ransomware operators: the adoption of ClickFix as a primary initial access vector.\n\nClickFix is a social engineering technique that has gained rapid adoption across the cybercriminal ecosystem since mid-2025. Unlike traditional phishing that depends on malicious attachments or links to exploit kits, ClickFix lures victims into manually executing malicious commands on their own systems. Targets are typically directed to a webpage — often disguised as a CAPTCHA verification, a document viewer, or a software update portal — that instructs them to open a Run dialog or terminal and paste a command, usually under the pretense of \"verifying\" their identity or fixing a display error.\n\nWhat makes this technique particularly effective is that it exploits user trust and bypasses automated email and web filtering controls entirely. The malicious payload is never delivered as a file attachment or a drive-by download; instead, the user becomes the execution mechanism. Security teams that have invested heavily in sandboxing, attachment scanning, and URL reputation analysis find their controls rendered irrelevant when the user willingly runs the payload themselves.\n\n## Technical Details\n\nIn the LeakNet campaign, the ClickFix lure pages have been observed masquerading as corporate document-sharing portals and video conferencing pre-join screens. Victims are presented with a fake loading error and instructed to press `Win+R`, then paste a PowerShell command that the page has silently copied to their clipboard. The command is a one-liner that downloads and executes the first-stage loader.\n\nThe first stage is where LeakNet's tooling diverges sharply from conventional ransomware infrastructure. Rather than deploying a compiled C/C++ or .NET binary — artifacts that endpoint detection and response (EDR) platforms are well-tuned to scrutinize — LeakNet has built its loader on the Deno runtime.\n\nDeno is a legitimate, open-source JavaScript and TypeScript runtime created by Ryan Dahl, the original creator of Node.js. It is designed with security-first principles, including a permissions-based model for file system, network, and environment access. Ironically, those same design characteristics make it attractive to threat actors. Deno ships as a single statically linked executable with no external dependencies, can bundle scripts into self-contained binaries, and is signed with a valid developer certificate. To most EDR platforms and application whitelisting solutions, a Deno-compiled binary appears as a benign, signed developer tool.\n\nThe LeakNet loader, compiled via `deno compile`, performs several functions: it establishes a persistent connection to the group's command-and-control (C2) infrastructure over HTTPS using WebSocket connections that blend with legitimate web traffic; it conducts host reconnaissance including domain enumeration, privilege assessment, and EDR product identification; and it downloads secondary payloads based on the target environment's profile. Researchers have observed the loader selectively deploying Cobalt Strike beacons, credential harvesting tools, and ultimately the LeakNet encryptor itself, which is a more conventional compiled payload that handles file encryption and ransom note delivery.\n\nThe use of Deno also complicates forensic analysis. JavaScript-based payloads embedded within a Deno binary are not as easily reverse-engineered as standard PE executables. Analysts must extract the bundled V8 snapshot or JavaScript source from the compiled artifact, a process that requires familiarity with Deno's internal bundling format — expertise that many incident response teams currently lack.\n\n## Real-World Impact\n\nThe operational impact has been significant. At least a dozen organizations across the manufacturing, legal services, and healthcare sectors have been confirmed as victims of this updated LeakNet campaign since early March 2026, according to data from the group's leak site and corroborated by incident response firms. Several of these intrusions progressed from initial access to domain-wide encryption in under 48 hours, a timeline that suggests a high degree of automation in the group's lateral movement and deployment tooling.\n\nThe ClickFix vector poses a particular challenge for organizations that have invested in security awareness training focused on traditional phishing. Employees trained to scrutinize email attachments and hover over links may not recognize the danger in a webpage instructing them to paste a command, especially when the lure page is professionally designed and contextually relevant to their work.\n\n## Threat Actor Context\n\nLeakNet operates under the standard double-extortion RaaS model, exfiltrating sensitive data before deploying encryption and threatening publication on a Tor-based leak site. The group is believed to be Russian-speaking based on language artifacts in early builds of its encryptor and its adherence to the common CIS-country exclusion list — systems configured with Russian, Ukrainian, Belarusian, or Kazakh keyboard layouts are left untouched.\n\nThe group's affiliate program appears to be actively recruiting, with posts on underground forums advertising a 70/30 revenue split in favor of affiliates. The adoption of ClickFix and the Deno-based toolchain may itself be a recruitment strategy, offering affiliates a turnkey initial access method that requires minimal technical sophistication to deploy while maintaining a lower detection rate than competing RaaS platforms.\n\nNotably, LeakNet is not the first ransomware operation to experiment with unconventional runtimes. Groups have previously leveraged Go, Rust, and even Nim to compile payloads that evade signature-based detection. However, the use of a JavaScript runtime represents a new frontier that security vendors are only beginning to address.\n\n## Defensive Recommendations\n\nSecurity teams should prioritize several defensive measures in response to this evolving threat:\n\n- **Block clipboard-based attacks**: Deploy browser-level controls or group policies that prevent web pages from writing to the system clipboard without explicit user consent. Modern browser security settings can restrict this behavior.\n- **Monitor for Deno execution**: Establish detection rules for `deno.exe` or Deno-compiled binaries executing in environments where the runtime is not part of the approved software inventory. Process creation logs and EDR telemetry should flag unsigned or anomalous Deno activity.\n- **Restrict PowerShell and Run dialog access**: For users who do not require administrative tooling, consider limiting access to PowerShell, `cmd.exe`, and the Run dialog through AppLocker or Windows Defender Application Control (WDAC) policies.\n- **Update security awareness training**: Educate employees specifically about ClickFix-style attacks. Training scenarios should include examples of fake CAPTCHA pages and document portals that instruct users to execute commands.\n- **Enhance network monitoring**: WebSocket-based C2 communications can be identified through network traffic analysis. Monitor for long-lived WebSocket connections to uncategorized or newly registered domains.\n- **Implement robust backup and recovery**: Ensure offline, immutable backups are tested regularly. A 48-hour dwell time leaves minimal margin for detection before encryption, making rapid recovery capabilities essential.\n\n## Industry Response\n\nMajor EDR vendors have begun issuing updated detection signatures targeting Deno-compiled binaries that exhibit malicious behavior patterns, though the inherent challenge of distinguishing malicious Deno applications from legitimate developer tools remains. YARA rules targeting the specific LeakNet loader variant have been published by multiple threat intelligence teams, and indicators of compromise including C2 domains and file hashes are being circulated through industry sharing platforms such as MISP and the Cyber Threat Alliance.\n\nThe Deno project maintainers have acknowledged awareness of the misuse but note that, like any general-purpose runtime, preventing all malicious use is not feasible. They have encouraged the security community to focus on behavioral detection rather than binary reputation, a recommendation that aligns with the broader industry shift toward behavior-based endpoint protection.\n\nAs ransomware operators continue to innovate in both social engineering and payload delivery, the LeakNet campaign serves as a reminder that defensive strategies must evolve beyond signature-based detection and traditional phishing awareness to address the increasingly creative methods threat actors employ to breach corporate networks.\n\n---\n\n**",
    "tldr": "** LeakNet ransomware now uses ClickFix social engineering to trick users into running malicious commands and deploys a Deno-based loader that evades EDR detection by disguising itself as a legitimate",
