    "headline": "Inside Olympic Cybersecurity: Lessons From Paris 2024 to Milan Cortina 2026",
    "summary": "Discover how Franz Regul, former CISO for the Paris 2024 Olympics, tackled unique cybersecurity challenges to protect the Olympics from evolving threats.",
    "body": "# Inside Olympic Cybersecurity: Lessons From Paris 2024 to Milan Cortina 2026\n\n## How One CISO Built a Cyber Defense Operation for the World's Largest Sporting Event — and What It Means for the Next Games\n\nWhen Franz Regul accepted the role of Chief Information Security Officer for the Paris 2024 Olympic and Paralympic Games, he inherited what may be the most complex temporary cybersecurity mandate on Earth: defend a sprawling, short-lived digital infrastructure against nation-state actors, hacktivists, cybercriminals, and opportunists — all while the entire world watches in real time.\n\nThe Paris 2024 Games, held across dozens of venues in July and August 2024, represented a massive attack surface. Ticketing platforms, broadcast systems, athlete databases, timing and scoring networks, credential management, transportation logistics, and public Wi-Fi all had to function flawlessly under intense scrutiny. A single visible compromise — a falsified score, a disrupted broadcast, a leaked athlete record — would have dominated global headlines within minutes.\n\nNow, with Milan Cortina 2026 on the horizon, the cybersecurity community is studying what Paris got right, what nearly went wrong, and how the next organizing committee can build on those lessons.\n\n## Background and Context\n\nThe Olympic Games have been a prime cyber target for over a decade. The 2018 PyeongChang Winter Olympics were hit by the infamous \"Olympic Destroyer\" malware during the opening ceremony, temporarily knocking out Wi-Fi, the official website, and broadcast feeds. Attribution later pointed to Russian military intelligence (GRU), operating under the Sandworm group, in what appeared to be retaliation for the doping ban imposed on Russian athletes.\n\nTokyo 2020, delayed to 2021 by the pandemic, reported over 450 million attempted cyberattacks during the Games — a figure that staggered even seasoned defenders. By the time Paris 2024 began planning its cybersecurity posture, the threat landscape had only intensified. The war in Ukraine had supercharged hacktivist activity. Ransomware-as-a-service operations had matured. AI-generated phishing campaigns were becoming indistinguishable from legitimate communications.\n\nRegul and his team at the Paris 2024 Organizing Committee, working closely with France's national cybersecurity agency (ANSSI), had to prepare for all of it — with the added constraint that the entire infrastructure would be built up, operated for weeks, and then torn down. There is no \"steady state\" in Olympic cybersecurity. Everything is temporary, which makes conventional enterprise security models difficult to apply directly.\n\n## Technical Details\n\nThe Paris 2024 cyber defense operation was built around several core principles that Regul has discussed publicly since the Games concluded.\n\n**Segmentation and zero trust architecture** formed the backbone. Critical systems — timing and scoring, broadcast distribution, and athlete management — were isolated on separate network segments with strict access controls. No implicit trust was granted based on network location. Every connection was authenticated and authorized independently, following zero trust principles that many enterprises still struggle to implement fully.\n\n**Threat intelligence sharing** was operationalized at scale. ANSSI stood up a dedicated Olympic threat intelligence cell that aggregated feeds from international partners, private sector vendors, and France's own signals intelligence capabilities. This allowed the security operations center (SOC) to prioritize alerts based on real-time geopolitical context — a critical capability when you are simultaneously monitoring for Russian state-sponsored intrusions, Chinese espionage campaigns, Iranian influence operations, and financially motivated ransomware crews.\n\n**Attack surface management** was relentless. The temporary nature of Olympic infrastructure means new systems, vendors, and integrations appear constantly in the months before the Games. Regul's team implemented continuous asset discovery and vulnerability scanning, with strict policies requiring remediation timelines tied to criticality scores. Shadow IT — unauthorized systems spun up by vendors or partner organizations — was treated as a top-tier risk.\n\n**Incident response rehearsals** went beyond tabletop exercises. The team conducted full-scale simulations that involved deliberately injecting realistic attack scenarios into pre-production environments, forcing the SOC and incident response teams to detect, triage, and contain threats under time pressure. These exercises exposed gaps in communication protocols and escalation paths that were corrected before the Games began.\n\nThe reported numbers from Paris 2024 tell the story: over 4 billion security events were processed during the Games, with the SOC triaging hundreds of confirmed incidents. Critically, none resulted in a publicly visible disruption to competition or broadcast operations.\n\n## Real-World Impact\n\nThe lessons from Paris 2024 extend well beyond sporting events. Any organization that operates large-scale temporary or rapidly changing infrastructure — think music festivals, political conventions, international summits, or even corporate mergers integrating IT environments — faces analogous challenges.\n\nThe principle of building security into ephemeral infrastructure from day one, rather than bolting it on after deployment, is universally applicable. So is the emphasis on pre-event adversary simulation. Many organizations conduct annual penetration tests or red team exercises; the Paris model of continuous, scenario-driven testing tied to real threat intelligence represents a higher standard that critical infrastructure operators should aspire to.\n\nThe dependency management challenge is equally transferable. The Olympics rely on hundreds of technology vendors, each introducing supply chain risk. Paris 2024's approach of treating every vendor integration as an attack vector — requiring security assessments, contractual obligations, and monitoring — mirrors the supply chain security frameworks that regulators worldwide are now mandating in the wake of incidents like SolarWinds and MOVEit.\n\n## Threat Actor Context\n\nThe Paris Games faced threats from the full spectrum of adversaries. Russian-affiliated groups remained a primary concern, given the historical precedent of Olympic Destroyer and the ongoing geopolitical tensions surrounding Ukraine. Hacktivist collectives aligned with various geopolitical causes targeted Olympic-adjacent infrastructure with DDoS campaigns and defacement attempts. Financially motivated actors attempted phishing campaigns targeting employees and volunteers, seeking credentials that could be monetized or used for further intrusion.\n\nANSSI publicly acknowledged that state-sponsored reconnaissance activity was detected against Olympic systems in the months leading up to the Games, though detailed attribution has not been fully disclosed. The sophistication of some observed tradecraft — including custom implants and living-off-the-land techniques designed to evade endpoint detection — indicated well-resourced threat actors with strategic objectives beyond simple disruption.\n\n## Defensive Recommendations\n\nSecurity professionals preparing for high-profile events or managing complex temporary environments should take several lessons from the Paris playbook:\n\n- **Adopt zero trust from the start.** Do not assume that temporary infrastructure is too short-lived to warrant proper access controls. Attackers know that time pressure leads to shortcuts.\n- **Invest in threat intelligence operationalization.** Raw feeds are insufficient. Dedicate resources to contextualizing intelligence against your specific threat model and attack surface.\n- **Conduct realistic adversary simulations.** Tabletop exercises have value, but full-scale technical simulations that stress-test detection and response capabilities reveal gaps that discussion alone cannot.\n- **Treat supply chain as a first-class risk.** Every vendor, contractor, and integration partner expands your attack surface. Assess, monitor, and enforce security requirements contractually.\n- **Plan for graceful degradation.** Assume that some attacks will succeed. Design systems so that a compromise in one segment does not cascade into a catastrophic failure across the entire operation.\n- **Build institutional knowledge transfer mechanisms.** The biggest risk for Milan Cortina 2026 is losing the hard-won lessons from Paris. Formal knowledge transfer programs, shared documentation, and personnel continuity are essential.\n\n## Industry Response\n\nThe cybersecurity community has been actively studying the Paris 2024 model. The International Olympic Committee (IOC) has expanded its cybersecurity working group and is facilitating structured knowledge transfer between the Paris and Milan Cortina organizing committees. ANSSI has published portions of its threat assessment methodology, contributing to the broader body of knowledge around event-based cybersecurity.\n\nVendors who participated in the Paris defense are packaging their experiences into frameworks and services aimed at other large-scale event operators. Several conference presentations and white papers from Paris SOC team members have circulated within the industry, detailing specific detection strategies and architectural decisions.\n\nFor Milan Cortina 2026, the Italian National Cybersecurity Agency (ACN) is already engaged in planning, with the advantage of a more defined threat landscape and a concrete operational model to build upon. The Winter Games present distinct challenges — geographically dispersed mountain venues with constrained connectivity, cold-weather infrastructure considerations, and a different vendor ecosystem — but the foundational principles proven in Paris provide a strong starting point.\n\nThe Olympic cybersecurity challenge remains one of the most demanding in the field: defend everything, for a fixed window, with global visibility, against the most capable adversaries on Earth. Paris 2024 demonstrated that it can be done. The question for Milan Cortina 2026 is whether those lessons will be preserved and adapted, or whether the next organizing committee will have to learn them again from scratch.\n\n---",
