    "summary": "CISA has added one new vulnerability to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities (KEV) Catalog, based on evidence of active exploitation.&nbsp; CVE-2025-47813 Wing FTP Server Information Disclosure Vulnerability This type of vulnerability is a frequent attack vector for malicious cyber actors and poses s",
    "body": "I wasn't able to search the web for additional details on this CVE. I'll write the article based on the provided information and established knowledge about Wing FTP Server, CISA's KEV program, and information disclosure vulnerabilities. Here's the article:\n\n---\n\n# CISA Adds Wing FTP Server Information Disclosure Flaw to Known Exploited Vulnerabilities Catalog\n\n## Active Exploitation Confirmed as Federal Agencies Face Remediation Deadline\n\nThe Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA) has added a new entry to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities (KEV) Catalog, flagging an information disclosure vulnerability in Wing FTP Server — tracked as **CVE-2025-47813** — that is being actively exploited in the wild. The addition signals that threat actors have moved beyond proof-of-concept and are leveraging the flaw in real-world attacks against organizations running the popular file transfer platform.\n\nThe KEV Catalog serves as CISA's authoritative record of vulnerabilities that have been confirmed as weaponized. Its significance extends beyond a simple advisory: under Binding Operational Directive (BOD) 22-01, all Federal Civilian Executive Branch (FCEB) agencies are required to remediate cataloged vulnerabilities within prescribed timelines. While the directive applies specifically to federal agencies, CISA strongly urges all organizations — public and private — to prioritize remediation of KEV-listed flaws as part of their vulnerability management programs.\n\n---\n\n## Background and Context\n\nWing FTP Server, developed by Wftpserver.com, is a cross-platform enterprise file transfer server that supports FTP, FTPS, SFTP, and HTTP/S protocols. It is widely deployed across small-to-midsize businesses, government agencies, and managed service providers for secure file exchange, automated transfers, and web-based file management. Its broad feature set and support for Windows, Linux, and macOS have made it a common fixture in environments that depend on reliable file transfer infrastructure.\n\nFile transfer platforms have become a favored target for threat actors in recent years. The exploitation of vulnerabilities in products like MOVEit Transfer, GoAnywhere MFT, and Accellion FTA demonstrated the devastating consequences of compromising file transfer infrastructure — often leading to mass data exfiltration, extortion, and supply chain breaches. The addition of CVE-2025-47813 to the KEV Catalog places Wing FTP Server squarely within this high-risk category and should prompt immediate attention from security teams.\n\nCISA's decision to catalog this vulnerability is based on confirmed evidence of active exploitation, meaning at least one threat actor has successfully weaponized the flaw against production systems. The agency does not add vulnerabilities to the KEV Catalog speculatively; inclusion requires verifiable proof that the issue is being exploited in real-world attack campaigns.\n\n---\n\n## Technical Details\n\nCVE-2025-47813 is classified as an **information disclosure vulnerability** in Wing FTP Server. Information disclosure flaws allow an attacker to access data that should not be exposed — this can range from configuration files and server metadata to user credentials, session tokens, directory structures, or even the contents of transferred files.\n\nWhile the full technical specifics of CVE-2025-47813 have not been exhaustively detailed in public disclosures at this time, information disclosure vulnerabilities in file transfer servers typically manifest through one or more of the following vectors:\n\n- **Path traversal or directory listing flaws** that permit unauthenticated or low-privileged users to enumerate or read files outside their designated scope.\n- **Improper access controls on administrative interfaces** that leak server configuration, user account details, or internal network information.\n- **API endpoint exposure** where web-based management interfaces return excessive data in responses, including sensitive system metadata.\n- **Log file or error message leakage** that inadvertently reveals credentials, file paths, or internal architecture details to unauthorized parties.\n\nIn the context of a file transfer server, information disclosure is particularly dangerous because the assets at risk — the files being transferred — often include sensitive business data, personally identifiable information (PII), financial records, healthcare data, or intellectual property. An attacker who successfully exploits this class of vulnerability may not need to escalate privileges further if the disclosed information itself holds significant value.\n\nMoreover, information disclosure frequently serves as a stepping stone in multi-stage attacks. Leaked credentials or configuration data can be used to gain authenticated access, pivot laterally within a network, or facilitate more destructive follow-on operations such as ransomware deployment or persistent backdoor installation.\n\n---\n\n## Real-World Impact\n\nThe implications of CVE-2025-47813 are significant for any organization running Wing FTP Server in its environment. Organizations that rely on Wing FTP Server for regulated data transfers — particularly those in healthcare, finance, government, and legal sectors — face heightened risk, as unauthorized data exposure in these verticals can trigger regulatory reporting obligations under frameworks like HIPAA, PCI-DSS, GDPR, and various state-level data breach notification laws.\n\nFor federal agencies, BOD 22-01 compliance mandates remediation within a defined timeline from the date of KEV Catalog addition. Failure to meet these deadlines is not merely a best-practice lapse; it constitutes a compliance violation subject to oversight and enforcement.\n\nThe broader enterprise landscape should treat the KEV listing as a high-priority signal. Organizations that have not inventoried their Wing FTP Server deployments — including shadow IT installations or instances managed by third-party vendors — should do so immediately. Internet-facing instances are at the greatest risk, but internal deployments are not immune, particularly in post-compromise scenarios where an attacker already has a foothold in the network.\n\n---\n\n## Threat Actor Context\n\nCISA has not publicly attributed the active exploitation of CVE-2025-47813 to a specific threat actor or group. However, the targeting of file transfer infrastructure aligns with tactics observed from both financially motivated cybercriminals and nation-state actors.\n\nThe Cl0p ransomware group, in particular, has demonstrated a pattern of exploiting zero-day and n-day vulnerabilities in file transfer platforms to conduct mass data theft and extortion campaigns. Their operations against MOVEit Transfer in 2023 affected over 2,500 organizations globally. While there is no confirmed link between Cl0p and CVE-2025-47813, the operational pattern — targeting widely deployed file transfer software for information access — is consistent with known threat actor playbooks.\n\nSecurity teams should monitor threat intelligence feeds for emerging indicators of compromise (IOCs) associated with this vulnerability and consider the possibility that exploitation may be opportunistic and widespread.\n\n---\n\n## Defensive Recommendations\n\nOrganizations running Wing FTP Server should take the following actions immediately:\n\n1. **Patch or update immediately.** Check with the vendor (wftpserver.com) for the latest security updates addressing CVE-2025-47813. Apply patches as the highest priority, particularly for internet-facing instances.\n\n2. **Inventory all instances.** Identify every Wing FTP Server deployment across the environment, including those managed by third parties or operating in non-production capacities.\n\n3. **Restrict network exposure.** If patching cannot be completed immediately, restrict access to Wing FTP Server instances by placing them behind VPN, allowlisting trusted IP ranges, and disabling unnecessary protocols or administrative interfaces.\n\n4. **Review logs for indicators of compromise.** Examine Wing FTP Server access logs, authentication logs, and system logs for anomalous activity — particularly unauthorized file access, unexpected directory traversal patterns, or access from unfamiliar IP addresses.\n\n5. **Assess data exposure.** Determine what data is stored on or transits through affected Wing FTP Server instances. If sensitive or regulated data may have been exposed, engage incident response and legal teams to assess notification obligations.\n\n6. **Monitor CISA advisories.** Continue tracking CISA's KEV Catalog and associated advisories for updated remediation guidance and IOCs.\n\n7. **Evaluate file transfer architecture.** Use this event as a catalyst to review whether legacy file transfer infrastructure meets current security requirements. Consider whether managed file transfer (MFT) solutions with stronger security postures are warranted.\n\n---\n\n## Industry Response\n\nThe security community has increasingly emphasized the systemic risk posed by file transfer platforms, and CISA's latest KEV addition reinforces that concern. Vulnerability researchers and defenders alike have called for organizations to treat file transfer servers as tier-one critical assets deserving the same security scrutiny as email gateways, VPN appliances, and identity providers.\n\nCISA's KEV Catalog has become one of the most operationally relevant resources in the vulnerability management ecosystem. With over 1,100 entries since its inception in November 2021, the catalog provides a curated, evidence-based signal that cuts through the noise of the tens of thousands of CVEs published annually. Security teams that integrate the KEV feed into their vulnerability prioritization workflows are better positioned to focus resources where real-world risk is highest.\n\nAs exploitation of file transfer infrastructure shows no sign of abating, the addition of CVE-2025-47813 to the KEV Catalog serves as a clear directive: organizations running Wing FTP Server must act now to assess their exposure and close this gap before adversaries exploit it further.\n\n---\n\n**",
